<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Altai Mountains</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Altai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Altai Mountains are a mountain range in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and Kazakhstan meet. The region was an important homeland and pastureland for nomadic peoples of the steppe. It is mentioned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Empire</a>'s profile in connection with the eastern lands contested during its decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/altai-mountains.jpg" alt="Altai Mountains" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rudyologist · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altai_Mountains.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…until their absorption by the Dzungars in the late seventeenth and early eighteenth centuries — but as an independent governing entity, the khanate that had once commanded the central Silk Road from the Amu Darya to the <b>Altai</b> had dissolved irreversibly into its nomadic and sedentary constituent parts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Khara Khula, chief of the Choros, built up a power base in the steppe between the <b>Altai</b> and the Ili River, and on his death in 1634 his son Erdeni Batur took the title of khong taiji and united most of the remaining Oirats.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Tongdian, says they were a branch of the Gaoju, early Turkic speakers, who came south from the <b>Altai</b> around 360, and the most prominent current view, that of de la Vaissière, makes them Turkic in origin, adopting the Bactrian language after settling in Bactria; others have argued…</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>By bringing much of the steppe between the Caspian Sea, the <b>Altai</b> and the Syr Darya under Kazakh khans, the khanate formed the space on which the borders of the Kazakh Soviet Socialist Republic, and in 1991 of independent Kazakhstan, were later drawn.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Subject groups such as the Gaoche and the Turks of the <b>Altai</b>, who worked iron for them, supplied livestock, warriors and metal, and the oasis states of Turfan and the Tarim Basin paid tribute and at times accepted Rouran-backed rulers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rouran-khaganate">Rouran Khaganate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Pazyryk tombs in the <b>Altai</b>, linked to the wider Saka-Scythian world, filled with ice soon after burial and so preserved the oldest known knotted pile carpet.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saka">Saka · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
